      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research inexpensive, sound rejection filters. Preserve the vertex-count check and identity fast path, then compare effective adjacency statistics: loop counts, sorted (loop bit, row-popcount) signatures, and component signatures. Explore joint color refinement using consistent signature identifiers across both graphs; unequal color-class populations prove non-isomorphism, but equality never proves isomorphism. Derive statistics from the Boolean adjacency matrix, not declared edge counts, because repeated edges collapse. Measure filters individually and retain only worthwhile stages. Export compatible vertex colors for downstream pruning.\",\"B\":\"Research faster exact verification while retaining the original Heap permutation sequence. Compare flat byte adjacency storage and an early-exit verifier against an incremental count of mismatched unordered vertex pairs, including diagonals. After swapping two images, update only pairs incident to those positions, without double counting. A zero mismatch count must be equivalent to the original verifier. Incremental O(n) updates can lose to early rejection, so measure both alternatives rather than assuming improvement. Also evaluate buffered output preserving every character and line. Use ARM64-compatible code under the unchanged compiler.\",\"C\":\"Research exact Heap-order subtree pruning. Express the original enumeration as recursive Heap blocks: a block permutes a prefix while its suffix remains fixed. Reject a block only when fixed suffix assignments violate loop, adjacency, or invariant compatibility. Crucially, skipping a block must apply its exact terminal permutation transformation so later blocks retain the reference order; derive these transformations by permutation composition without enumerating factorially many leaves. Preserve the initial identity-only return, and otherwise emit every valid mapping in original order. Compare simple suffix pruning with stronger sound candidate-domain checks, accounting for their overhead.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only validated programs produced by A/B/C. Measure standalone winners and selected combinations through the unchanged correctness and timing harness; gains are not additive. In particular, B's incremental mismatch state becomes stale after C's skipped-block transformation unless explicitly restored. Prefer retaining separate variants over an unproven combination. Require semantic justification beyond the four observed correctness cases, covering loops, collapsed duplicate edges, disconnected graphs, unequal sizes, identity matches, and multiple nonidentity matches. Preserve exact output formatting and order. Keep the fastest validated measured candidate, including the baseline if necessary. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions, or specialize to test values.\",\"analysis\":\"Only Problem A and src/brute_isomorphism.cpp are relevant. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 0.008430607 seconds, with samples spanning approximately 0.00828–0.00865 seconds. No case sizes or profiling are supplied, so factorial enumeration is a source-derived concern, not an established measured bottleneck; startup and optimization overhead may matter substantially. The crucial reference behavior is unusual: an identity isomorphism prints once and returns, but otherwise every matching permutation is printed in Heap order, followed by no failure message. If none match, it prints the exact failure line. Returning an arbitrary isomorphism or stopping at the first nonidentity match would change behavior. The representation also preserves self-loops and collapses repeated edges. The three research lanes therefore separate invariant rejection, hot-loop engineering, and order-preserving search pruning. Start with low-overhead serial implementations on the four-core ARM64 CPU; there is no GPU.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"48c9afd779656c5d672bacfa846c82c8b4ab8de288d1378203d558bdea5d3766\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research inexpensive, sound rejection filters. Preserve the vertex-count check and identity fast path, then compare effective adjacency statistics: loop counts, sorted (loop bit, row-popcount) signatures, and component signatures. Explore joint color refinement using consistent signature identifiers across both graphs; unequal color-class populations prove non-isomorphism, but equality never proves isomorphism. Derive statistics from the Boolean adjacency matrix, not declared edge counts, because repeated edges collapse. Measure filters individually and retain only worthwhile stages. Export compatible vertex colors for downstream pruning.\",\"B\":\"Research faster exact verification while retaining the original Heap permutation sequence. Compare flat byte adjacency storage and an early-exit verifier against an incremental count of mismatched unordered vertex pairs, including diagonals. After swapping two images, update only pairs incident to those positions, without double counting. A zero mismatch count must be equivalent to the original verifier. Incremental O(n) updates can lose to early rejection, so measure both alternatives rather than assuming improvement. Also evaluate buffered output preserving every character and line. Use ARM64-compatible code under the unchanged compiler.\",\"C\":\"Research exact Heap-order subtree pruning. Express the original enumeration as recursive Heap blocks: a block permutes a prefix while its suffix remains fixed. Reject a block only when fixed suffix assignments violate loop, adjacency, or invariant compatibility. Crucially, skipping a block must apply its exact terminal permutation transformation so later blocks retain the reference order; derive these transformations by permutation composition without enumerating factorially many leaves. Preserve the initial identity-only return, and otherwise emit every valid mapping in original order. Compare simple suffix pruning with stronger sound candidate-domain checks, accounting for their overhead.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only validated programs produced by A/B/C. Measure standalone winners and selected combinations through the unchanged correctness and timing harness; gains are not additive. In particular, B's incremental mismatch state becomes stale after C's skipped-block transformation unless explicitly restored. Prefer retaining separate variants over an unproven combination. Require semantic justification beyond the four observed correctness cases, covering loops, collapsed duplicate edges, disconnected graphs, unequal sizes, identity matches, and multiple nonidentity matches. Preserve exact output formatting and order. Keep the fastest validated measured candidate, including the baseline if necessary. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions, or specialize to test values.\",\"analysis\":\"Only Problem A and src/brute_isomorphism.cpp are relevant. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 0.008430607 seconds, with samples spanning approximately 0.00828–0.00865 seconds. No case sizes or profiling are supplied, so factorial enumeration is a source-derived concern, not an established measured bottleneck; startup and optimization overhead may matter substantially. The crucial reference behavior is unusual: an identity isomorphism prints once and returns, but otherwise every matching permutation is printed in Heap order, followed by no failure message. If none match, it prints the exact failure line. Returning an arbitrary isomorphism or stopping at the first nonidentity match would change behavior. The representation also preserves self-loops and collapses repeated edges. The three research lanes therefore separate invariant rejection, hot-loop engineering, and order-preserving search pruning. Start with low-overhead serial implementations on the four-core ARM64 CPU; there is no GPU.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-astra\",\"nonce\":\"9d4270415ecd40039c24c8359450500d\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"xhigh\",\"request_sha256\":\"48c9afd779656c5d672bacfa846c82c8b4ab8de288d1378203d558bdea5d3766\",\"session_id\":\"2bd8b683b45b41c8b48ec38708f46bab\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"8b9c883d6e2b48a7247d88b85df58e8a1b3104505b3a42d3f29bdeca7d436140\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":11456,\"output_tokens\":3283,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":2588}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-astra\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"xhigh\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":14739,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
